# DAX (Data Analysis Expressions)

## Introduction to DAX

DAX (Data Analysis Expressions) is a formula language used in Microsoft Power BI,
Excel Power Pivot, and SQL Server Analysis Services (SSAS).
It is mainly used to perform calculations, create custom columns, build measures, 
and analyze data in business reports.

DAX helps users create intelligent reports by using formulas similar to Excel
formulas but with more powerful analytical capabilities.

---

# Features of DAX

* Used for data analysis and calculations
* Works with tables and relationships
* Supports dynamic filtering
* Provides time-based analysis
* Helps create KPIs and dashboards
* Supports advanced business logic

---

# Components of DAX

DAX formulas mainly contain:

* Functions
* Operators
* Constants
* References

### Example

```DAX id="oq5c8m"
Profit = Sales[Revenue] - Sales[Cost]
```

Here:

* `Profit` → Measure name
* `Sales[Revenue]` → Column reference
* `-` → Arithmetic operator

---

# Calculated Columns

## Definition

A Calculated Column is a new column created using DAX formulas.
The formula is calculated row by row and values are stored permanently in memory.

Calculated columns are mainly used for:

* Data categorization
* Data transformation
* Combining fields
* Creating custom attributes

---

## Syntax

```DAX id="z0nl7e"
Column_Name = Formula
```

---

## Example 1: Full Name Column

```DAX id="m4cxzn"
Full_Name =
Employees[FirstName] & " " & Employees[LastName]
```

### Explanation

* `&` joins text values
* Creates a full name by combining first and last names

---

## Example 2: Product Category

```DAX id="w5p0b0"
Category =
IF(Products[Price] > 1000, "Premium", "Standard")
```

### Explanation

* If product price is greater than 1000 → Premium
* Otherwise → Standard

---

## Advantages of Calculated Columns

* Useful for grouping data
* Can be used in slicers and filters
* Easy to create custom classifications

---

## Limitations

* Increases model size
* Uses memory because values are stored physically

---

# Measures

## Definition

Measures are dynamic calculations used for aggregations such as:

* Sum
* Average
* Count
* Profit
* Percentage

Measures are calculated only when required inside visuals.

---

## Syntax

```DAX id="ewll8m"
Measure_Name = Formula
```

---

## Example 1: Total Sales

```DAX id="e5nmdo"
Total_Sales = SUM(Sales[Amount])
```

### Explanation

* SUM function adds all sales amounts

---

## Example 2: Profit Margin

```DAX id="ewm4an"
Profit_Margin =
DIVIDE(
SUM(Sales[Profit]),
SUM(Sales[Revenue])
)
```

### Explanation

* DIVIDE safely divides values
* Avoids divide-by-zero errors

---

## Advantages of Measures

* Faster performance
* Consumes less memory
* Dynamic calculations
* Best for reports and charts

---

# Difference Between Calculated Columns and Measures

| Feature     | Calculated Column | Measure             |
| ----------- | ----------------- | ------------------- |
| Calculation | Row by Row        | Aggregated          |
| Storage     | Stored in memory  | Not stored          |
| Performance | Slower            | Faster              |
| Usage       | Filters/Slicers   | Visual Calculations |

---

# Calculated Tables

## Definition

Calculated Tables are tables created using DAX formulas instead of importing data directly.

They are useful for:

* Summary tables
* Filtered tables
* Intermediate calculations

---

## Syntax

```DAX id="lmfx0w"
Table_Name = Formula
```

---

## Example

```DAX id="z8p7v4"
Top_Customers =
FILTER(Customers, Customers[Sales] > 50000)
```

### Explanation

* Creates a new table containing customers with sales above 50000

