# Date & Time Functions

Used for date calculations and analysis.

---

## TODAY

Returns current date.

```DAX id="qx1k1u"
TodayDate = TODAY()
```

---

## NOW

Returns current date and time.

```DAX id="bwt95y"
CurrentDateTime = NOW()
```

---

## YEAR

Returns year from date.

```DAX id="1z90y5"
Year =
YEAR(Sales[OrderDate])
```

---

## MONTH

Returns month number.

```DAX id="mup8j5"
Month =
MONTH(Sales[OrderDate])
```

---

## DAY

Returns day value.

```DAX id="jlwmq1"
Day =
DAY(Sales[OrderDate])
```

---

## DATEDIFF

Calculates difference between dates.

```DAX id="1hf53l"
DaysDifference =
DATEDIFF(
Sales[StartDate],
Sales[EndDate],
DAY
)
```

---

# Filter Functions

Filter functions control filter context.

---

## FILTER

Returns filtered table.

```DAX id="95zj2o"
HighSales =
FILTER(Sales, Sales[Amount] > 1000)
```

---

## CALCULATE

Changes filter context.

```DAX id="0xqqxt"
WestSales =
CALCULATE(
SUM(Sales[Amount]),
Region[Name] = "West"
)
```

### Explanation

* Calculates sales only for West region

---

## ALL

Removes filters.

```DAX id="g8tv6i"
TotalSales =
CALCULATE(
SUM(Sales[Amount]),
ALL(Sales)
)
```

---

## VALUES

Returns unique values.

```DAX id="n2omdr"
VALUES(Product[Category])
```

---

## SELECTEDVALUE

Returns selected value from slicer.

```DAX id="gr8p4l"
SelectedRegion =
SELECTEDVALUE(Region[Name])
```

---

# Time Intelligence Functions

Time intelligence functions perform date-based calculations.

## Requirements

* Proper Date Table
* Continuous dates
* Relationship with fact table

---

# Creating Date Table

```DAX id="jlwm4s"
DateTable =
CALENDAR(
DATE(2024,1,1),
DATE(2025,12,31)
)
```

---

## TOTALYTD

Calculates Year-To-Date values.

```DAX id="jlwmvh"
YTD_Sales =
TOTALYTD(
SUM(Sales[Amount]),
DateTable[Date]
)
```

---

## TOTALMTD

Calculates Month-To-Date values.

```DAX id="g5k5fi"
MTD_Sales =
TOTALMTD(
SUM(Sales[Amount]),
DateTable[Date]
)
```

---

## TOTALQTD

Calculates Quarter-To-Date values.

```DAX id="ezc4qj"
QTD_Sales =
TOTALQTD(
SUM(Sales[Amount]),
DateTable[Date]
)
```

---

## SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR

Returns same period from previous year.

```DAX id="jlwmji"
LastYearSales =
CALCULATE(
SUM(Sales[Amount]),
SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR(DateTable[Date])
)
```

---

## DATEADD

Shifts dates forward or backward.

```DAX id="fd6m9k"
PreviousMonthSales =
CALCULATE(
SUM(Sales[Amount]),
DATEADD(DateTable[Date], -1, MONTH)
)
```

---

# Variables in DAX

## Definition

Variables store temporary values inside formulas.

Variables improve:

* Readability
* Performance
* Formula management

---

## Syntax

```DAX id="jlwmkn"
VAR variable_name = value
RETURN result
```

---

## Example

```DAX id="6tr6u6"
NetProfit =
VAR Revenue = SUM(Sales[Revenue])
VAR Cost = SUM(Sales[Cost])

RETURN Revenue - Cost
```

---

## Benefits of Variables

* Reduces repeated calculations
* Makes formulas cleaner
* Improves execution speed
* Easier debugging

---

# Row Context and Filter Context

## Row Context

* Works row by row
* Mostly used in Calculated Columns

### Example

```DAX id="jlwm1a"
Tax =
Sales[Amount] * 0.18
```

---

## Filter Context

* Applies filters dynamically
* Mainly used in Measures

### Example

```DAX id="jlwm2b"
WestSales =
CALCULATE(
SUM(Sales[Amount]),
Region[Name] = "West"
)
```

---

# Best Practices for DAX

## Use Measures Instead of Calculated Columns

Measures improve performance and reduce memory usage.

---

## Use Variables

Variables simplify complex formulas.

---

## Avoid Deep Nested IF Statements

Use SWITCH for better readability.

---

## Use DIVIDE Instead of /

DIVIDE prevents divide-by-zero errors.

---

## Create Proper Date Tables

Important for Time Intelligence calculations.

---

# Advantages of DAX

* Powerful analytical calculations
* Dynamic reporting
* Better business insights
* Advanced filtering capabilities
* Real-time data analysis

---

